# Identifiers for axis E2 of the portfolio audit — `0010` §3.2.
#
# TWO FILES, ONE TEXT, AND WHICH RULE APPLIES DEPENDS ON THE NAME YOU HAVE OPEN.
# A `cp` copies this header along with everything else, so it is written to be true in
# both places:
#
#   audit-identifiers.local.example   the template. TRACKED — committed to the repository.
#                                     Never type an identifier into it. Copy it first:
#
#                                       cp audit-identifiers.local.example audit-identifiers.local
#
#   audit-identifiers.local           the working copy the audit reads. IGNORED and never
#                                     committed. THIS IS THE ONE YOU FILL IN.
#
# ONE LOCATION, AND THE REASON IS NOT TIDINESS. The working copy belongs at the root of
# THIS repository — `current_projects` — and nowhere else. `.gitignore` is this repository's
# file and git does not descend into submodules, so the same filename inside `ab-lab/`,
# `car-price-ml/` or any other sibling is ignored by NOTHING, and every one of those twelve
# is a PUBLIC repository. Sessions 1–12 each work with a submodule directory open, which is
# exactly where a bare relative `cp` lands wrong.
# Verified: `git -C ab-lab check-ignore audit-identifiers.local` exits 1.
#
# Committing the template is safe by construction: it holds placeholders and no identifier,
# and the ignore pattern names an exact filename, so it does not match the `.example`.
# `git check-ignore` answers the ignore half, `git ls-files` the tracking half, and
# `tests/test_audit_identifiers.py` keeps both true after the next edit rather than at the
# moment of this one.
#
# FORM. One `key = value` per line, UTF-8; blank lines and `#` comments ignored.
# Keys are free text — the sweep uses the VALUES, so add or drop lines freely.
#
# A line still carrying `<fill in or delete this line>` supplies nothing, and a file where
# NO line supplies a value leaves axis E2 `blocked` — the same verdict as if it were absent.
# That is deliberate: a sweep for nothing returns clean, and an axis marked clean that was
# not checked is worse than one left open. A category that does not apply to you should have
# its line DELETED rather than left as a placeholder.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT BELONG HERE. The git author name and e-mail: `0010` §3.2 takes those from
# `git config`, and they are already measured as R-1. This file is for the private half.
#
# WHY THE FILLED FILE IS NEVER COMMITTED. §3.2's own sentence: writing a PESEL or a postal
# address into a document would create the exposure the audit exists to find. The audit
# searches for the owner's identifiers without any of them entering the record — a row in
# §4 says how many values were swept, never which.

private_email  = <fill in or delete this line>
phone          = <fill in or delete this line>
address_street = <fill in or delete this line>
address_city   = <fill in or delete this line>
address_postal = <fill in or delete this line>
employer_1     = <fill in or delete this line>
employer_2     = <fill in or delete this line>
university     = <fill in or delete this line>

# Add anything else worth sweeping for — an old handle, a second e-mail, a client name that
# appears in a project's data. One line each. National-ID and bank-shaped numbers are NOT
# written here: §3.2 matches those by shape, never by value.
